art_direction.glass_treatment:
**A floating pill, not a full-bleed bar — and the smaller area is both the better look and a ~70% cut in the dominant performance term. Take the free win.**

**Geometry.** Desktop: centred, `max-width: 560px`, `height: 52px`, `top: 20px`, `border-radius: 999px`. Contents, five words total, Palace's cap: `[OT monogram] · SHOP · DROP · WORLD · BAG(2)`, with everything else behind a `MENU` opener. Mobile: `inset-inline: 12px`, `height: 48px`, three hit targets only — `[OT monogram] · BAG(2) · MENU`. The wrapper is `pointer-events: none` with the pill `pointer-events: auto`, so the page behind stays clickable. **The thin OUR TIME wordmark never appears at nav scale** — only the heavy OT monogram, which is what monograms are for.

**THE LADDER. Tier 2 is the product. Tier 1 is a garnish. Tier 3 is the floor.**

**Tier 3 — universal floor, no backdrop-filter at all** (old browsers, `prefers-contrast: more`, `forced-colors`, and the deliberate opaque state over the hero):
```css
.ot-nav { background: color-mix(in oklab, #0F0F0F 92%, transparent);
          border: 1px solid rgb(250 246 246 / .14);
          box-shadow: 0 8px 32px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / .5); }
```

**Tier 2 — THE DESIGN. Safari, Firefox, every phone. Art-direct until *this* looks finished on an iPhone.** Three stacked layers, not one:
```css
@supports ((backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px))) {
 .ot-nav {
   background:
     linear-gradient(180deg, rgb(250 246 246 / .10), rgb(250 246 246 / .03) 42%, rgb(250 246 246 / .07)),
     rgb(15 15 15 / var(--ot-scrim, .74));            /* the contrast FLOOR, locked token */
   -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(180%);
           backdrop-filter: blur(18px) saturate(180%);
   box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 0 rgb(250 246 246 / .38),
               inset 0 -1px 0 0 rgb(250 246 246 / .10),
               0 8px 32px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / .45);
 }
}
@media (hover: none) and (pointer: coarse) {
 .ot-nav { -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(12px) saturate(160%);
                   backdrop-filter: blur(12px) saturate(160%); }
}
```
`saturate(180%)` is doing more work than the blur — glass concentrates colour; without it you get fog. Blur capped at 18px desktop / 12px mobile; above ~20px low-end devices degrade sharply for no visual gain.

**Tier 1 — real refraction, Chromium desktop only, and it must be invisible when absent.** `backdrop-filter: url(#ot-refract)` with an SDF-derived rounded-rect displacement map (kube.io's maths, PallavAg's `userSpaceOnUse` handling), `color-interpolation-filters="sRGB"`, `filterUnits="userSpaceOnUse"` with the region recomputed on resize, both `href` and `xlink:href` on `feImage`. **Chromatic dispersion is omitted entirely** — RGB fringing reads as glitch, which is the exact opposite of washed vintage-athletic. Gated by an engine probe, not `@supports`, because Firefox returns true and then silently renders nothing:
```js
const apple = /iPhone|iPad|iPod/.test(navigator.platform) ||
              (navigator.platform === 'MacIntel' && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 1);
const chromium = (navigator.userAgentData?.brands ?? []).some(b => b.brand === 'Chromium');
const roomy = matchMedia('(min-width:900px) and (pointer:fine)').matches;
```
**Design rule: if Tier 1 never ships in WebKit, nothing is missing.** The identity is entirely carried by Tier 2.

**THE THREE BEHAVIOURS THAT MAKE IT BESPOKE**

1. **It goes solid over the hero.** While the pinned sequence is on screen, the pill is Tier 3 solid. Two pre-blurred layers cross-fade on `opacity` (240ms linear) at the pin boundary — never a transitioning blur radius. This is the single most important performance decision on the site (blur over a per-frame-repainting canvas is the most expensive combination available) and it is simultaneously the concept's punctuation.

2. **It knows what it is over.** A single `IntersectionObserver` with `rootMargin: '-20px 0px -92% 0px'` — a sliver of viewport exactly where the pill sits — reads `data-nav-theme` off each section and flips the pill's whole token set. Over the sand WORLD section it becomes `data-over="light"`: ink `#0F0F0F`, scrim `rgb(207 194 180 / .70)`, rim `rgb(255 255 255 / .85)`. This is Represent's `data-style` move done properly, it is Apple's own adaptive-tint ingredient achieved with zero unsupported CSS, and **the same observer drives the solid-over-video switch** — one mechanism, two payoffs.

3. **The specular rakes with scroll.** A masked conic-gradient border ring whose angle is a registered `@property --ot-light: <angle>`, driven from sand level during the pin and page progress after it. The highlight crosses the pill's rim like a second hand crossing a watch face. Works in Chrome, Safari and Firefox identically, costs nothing, and is the detail that reads as art-directed rather than generated.

**Accessibility, stated honestly.** `prefers-reduced-transparency` is **not supported in Safari** — the exact platform whose users are most likely to have Reduce Transparency on, because Apple shipped it as the remedy for Liquid Glass legibility complaints. So it cannot be the safety net. The `--ot-scrim: .74` floor is the real protection: bone `#FAF6F6` over 74% `#0F0F0F` clears 4.5:1 regardless of what is behind the pill. `prefers-contrast: more` (Chrome 96+, FF 101+, **Safari 14.1+**) is the only working lever on Apple platforms and it drops the pill to Tier 3 solid with a `currentColor` border. `forced-colors: active` → `Canvas`/`CanvasText`. Focus rings are solid bone, 2px, rendered above the glass. Apple itself is dialling transparency down in iOS 27 — build restrained.

**Glass appears in exactly three places on the entire site:** the nav pill, the sticky filter bar on collection pages (the one place the category actually does this — Sporty & Rich), and the cart-drawer scrim. Never on a card. A `backdrop-blur` card grid is tell #23.
